# Prisma supports the native connection string format for PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite, SQL Server, MongoDB and CockroachDB.
# See the documentation for all the connection string options: https://pris.ly/d/connection-strings

# ── DATABASE ──
# Supabase Postgres — the same project as the auth keys below. Supabase ships a
# Postgres database with every project, so running a separate one was two
# services doing one job; Neon was dropped on 2026-09-11.
#
# Supabase > Project Settings > Database > Connection string.
#
# USE THE POOLER, ON PORT 5432. Three strings are offered and only one is right:
#   - Direct (db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432)        — works locally, runs out of
#     connections in production, since every serverless instance opens its own.
#   - Pooler, transaction mode (port 6543)      — pooled, but hands the
#     connection back after each statement, which breaks the interactive
#     transactions in /api/log and /api/favorites.
#   - Pooler, session mode (port 5432)          — THIS ONE.
#
# The pooler's username is postgres.<project-ref>, not plain postgres. Copying
# the direct string's username onto the pooler host fails like a wrong password.
#
# `npm run check:db` checks all of the above and says which you have.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"

# PODCAST_INDEX_API_KEY / _SECRET and SESSION_SECRET were documented here and
# read by nothing (removed 2026-09-10). The Podcast Index API was replaced by
# iTunes, and sessions are Supabase's now. They sent people off to sign up for
# credentials the app never used.

# ── SUPABASE AUTH ──
# Required. Without both, nobody can sign in and every visitor is treated as
# logged out — the rest of the site still renders.
#
# Both come from your Supabase project: Settings -> API. Same project as
# DATABASE_URL above.
# The anon key is meant to be public — it carries no privileges on its own, and
# access is governed by Row Level Security policies. The SERVICE ROLE key is not
# this key: never put that one in a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable, since anything with
# that prefix is compiled into the JavaScript sent to browsers.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=""

# The site's public origin, used to build password-reset links.
# Optional locally (falls back to the request's own origin). REQUIRED in
# production: behind Vercel's TLS termination the request the server sees can
# carry http://, which puts a downgraded link in people's inboxes and is
# refused by Supabase's redirect allow-list. No trailing slash.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=""
